export function fullKafkaArchitectContent() {
  return `<article class="rate-guide kafka-guide">
    <nav class="topic-jump kafka-jump" aria-label="Kafka architect page sections"><span>INTERVIEW GUIDE</span><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-problem">1. Problem</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-math">2. Capacity math</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-architecture">3. Architecture</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-multitenancy">4. Multi-tenancy</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-burst">5. Burst handling</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-quotas">6. Quotas</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-partitioning">7. Partitioning</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-sizing">8. Partitions</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-brokers">9. Brokers</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-consumers">10. Consumers</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-failures">11. Failures</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-dr">12. DR</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-schema">13. Schemas</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-replay">14. Replay</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-controlplane">15. Control plane</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-avoid">16. What not to do</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-questions">17. Follow-ups</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-answer">18. 2-minute answer</button><button type="button" data-scroll-to="ka-links">19. References</button></nav>
    <section class="revision-card kafka-hero" id="ka-problem"><span class="revision-kicker">ARCHITECT / STAFF+ · 100M EVENTS/SEC · MULTI-TENANT</span><h2>The interview problem</h2><p><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a Kafka platform for 100M events/sec. Multiple tenants produce events and have downstream consumers. One tenant suddenly has a huge burst. Prevent the noisy neighbor from affecting everyone else and explain what you would re-architect.</p><p class="callout"><strong>Architect mindset:</strong> do not jump straight to “add brokers.” Treat this as capacity, admission control, tenant isolation, buffering, downstream protection, and control-plane design.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-math"><div class="guide-title"><span>02</span><div><h2>Start with the capacity math</h2><p>Expose the physical cost before drawing boxes.</p></div></div><div class="capacity-grid"><div><b>100M/s</b><span>peak event rate</span></div><div><b>≈100 GB/s</b><span>logical ingress at 1 KB/event</span></div><div><b>≈8.64 PB/day</b><span>raw logical data</span></div><div><b>≈25.9 PB/day</b><span>RF=3 before compression and overhead</span></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>100M events/s × 1 KB ≈ 100 GB/s<br>100 GB/s × 86,400 ≈ 8.64 PB/day<br>8.64 PB/day × RF(3) ≈ 25.9 PB/day</code></pre><p class="callout"><strong>Interview point:</strong> Kafka is the hot, replayable event backbone. Object storage or a data lake is the cheaper long-term archive.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-architecture"><div class="guide-title"><span>03</span><div><h2>High-level architecture</h2><p>Shared by default, quota protected, burst tolerant, selectively isolated.</p></div></div><figure class="system-diagram kafka-architecture"><figcaption><strong>Multi-tenant streaming platform</strong><span>Admission before shared infrastructure.</span></figcaption><div class="kafka-control"><span class="plane-label">CONTROL PLANE</span><b>Tenant registry</b><i>Policy + quotas</i><i>Capacity manager</i><i>Placement + promotion</i></div><div class="kafka-flow"><span class="plane-label">DATA PLANE</span><div>Tenant ingestion<small>identify + authenticate</small></div><b>→</b><div class="accent">Admission + router<small>rate limit · buffer · route</small></div><b>→</b><div>Shared A / B<small>small + medium</small></div><div>Dedicated Kafka<small>large + extreme</small></div><b>→</b><div>Stream processing<small>shared + isolated pools</small></div><b>→</b><div>Destinations<small>lake · DB · index</small></div></div><p class="diagram-note"><strong>Cross-region:</strong> asynchronously replicate selected critical topics for DR.</p></figure></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-multitenancy"><div class="guide-title"><span>04</span><div><h2>Multi-tenant isolation model</h2><p>Isolation spans access, namespace, performance, compute, and failure domains.</p></div></div><div class="tier-table four-col"><div class="table-head"><span>Tier</span><span>Characteristics</span><span>Placement</span><span>Protection</span></div><div><b>Small</b><span>Predictable, low volume</span><span>Shared cluster</span><span>Kafka quotas + shared consumers</span></div><div><b>Medium</b><span>Higher steady rate</span><span>Shared + stronger reservation</span><span>Quotas + partitions + autoscaling</span></div><div><b>Large</b><span>High volume / strict SLA</span><span>Isolated pool or cluster</span><span>Dedicated capacity</span></div><div><b>Extreme</b><span>Huge, bursty, unpredictable</span><span>Dedicated cluster</span><span>Brokers + consumers + policy</span></div></div><div class="isolation-list"><span><b>Access</b> authentication / ACLs</span><span><b>Namespace</b> topics / logical namespaces</span><span><b>Performance</b> quotas / reservations</span><span><b>Compute</b> consumer pools / node pools</span><span><b>Failure</b> dedicated clusters / regions</span></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-burst"><div class="guide-title"><span>05</span><div><h2>What happens when one tenant bursts?</h2><p>Classify short-lived pressure separately from sustained growth.</p></div></div><div class="burst-diagram"><div><b>Tenant A</b><span>500 MB/s → 8 GB/s</span></div><i>↓</i><div class="accent"><b>Admission control</b><span>quota + priority</span></div><i>↓</i><div class="burst-split"><span><b>Within limit</b> → Kafka</span><span><b>Exceeds limit</b> → buffer / throttle</span></div><i>↓</i><div class="burst-split"><span><b>Short-lived</b> → drain burst</span><span><b>Sustained</b> → promote / isolate</span></div></div><div class="burst-path"><div><b>Short burst</b><span>Use quotas, backpressure, and buffering. Do not migrate infrastructure for every spike.</span></div><div><b>Sustained high volume</b><span>Promote to dedicated topics, partition pools, or a dedicated cluster.</span></div><div><b>Capacity invariant</b><span>At 10 GB/s ingress and 2 GB/s drain, backlog grows 8 GB/s.</span></div></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-quotas"><div class="guide-title"><span>06</span><div><h2>Kafka quotas</h2><p>The first line of defense for shared broker resources.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>Tenant A → 500 MB/s producer quota<br>Tenant B → 200 MB/s producer quota<br>Tenant C →   1 GB/s producer quota</code></pre><p class="callout warning"><strong>Do not overclaim:</strong> quotas do not solve business-aware burst absorption, downstream overload, or automatic tenant promotion.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="ka-partitioning"><div><div class="guide-title"><span>07</span><div><h2>Partitioning and ordering</h2><p>Parallelism follows the business key.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>partition = hash(customerId) % N</code></pre><p>Ordering is preserved per customer while customers process in parallel. A single hot customer can pin one partition; sharding it restores scale but changes ordering semantics.</p></div><div id="ka-sizing"><div class="guide-title"><span>08</span><div><h2>Partition sizing</h2><p>Benchmark the real workload.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>required partitions ≈ total throughput<br>                      ÷ sustainable throughput/partition<br><br>100 GB/s ÷ 25 MB/s ≈ 4,000 partitions</code></pre><p>The example is illustrative. Add headroom, account for skew, and validate recovery behavior.</p></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="ka-brokers"><div><div class="guide-title"><span>09</span><div><h2>Broker sizing</h2><p>Size to the limiting resource.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>fleet = max(network, disk, CPU)<br>        × replication / recovery headroom</code></pre><p>Benchmark peak traffic, RF=3, normal consumers, and broker or AZ failure. A fleet at 95% steady state cannot recover safely.</p></div><div id="ka-consumers"><div class="guide-title"><span>10</span><div><h2>Consumer isolation</h2><p>Do not move the outage downstream.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>Kafka ─┬→ shared consumer pool<br>       └→ dedicated consumer pool<br><br>consumer → bulkhead / rate limit → DB / API</code></pre><p>Consumer autoscaling must respect database, API, search, and index capacity.</p></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-failures"><div class="guide-title"><span>11</span><div><h2>Failure scenarios</h2><p>Plan recovery capacity and business-level idempotency.</p></div></div><div class="deep-dive-list"><details open><summary><span>Broker failure</span><em>leadership + recovery</em></summary><p>A replica becomes leader and clients reconnect. Recovery consumes disk and network, so reserve recovery capacity.</p></details><details><summary><span>Availability-zone failure</span><em>failure-domain spread</em></summary><p>Spread replicas across failure domains. Surviving brokers need enough spare capacity to absorb the lost zone.</p></details><details><summary><span>Consumer crash after DB commit</span><em>duplicate processing</em></summary><p>The event may run again. Use stable event IDs and idempotent business processing; offsets alone do not eliminate duplicates.</p></details><details><summary><span>Poison message</span><em>diagnose + replay</em></summary><p>Send repeated failures to a DLQ with topic, partition, offset, event ID, error, schema version, and original payload.</p></details><details><summary><span>Downstream DB slower than Kafka</span><em>durable buffer</em></summary><p>Buffer between stages, but monitor backlog age and retention so the queue does not silently exhaust.</p></details></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="ka-dr"><div><div class="guide-title"><span>12</span><div><h2>Multi-region DR</h2><p>Measure the promised RPO.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>US Kafka  ═══ async ═══▶  EU Kafka<br>EU Kafka  ═══ async ═══▶  US Kafka</code></pre><p>Use regional ingestion and replicate only critical topics. A one-minute RPO requires continuous replication-lag monitoring.</p></div><div id="ka-schema"><div class="guide-title"><span>13</span><div><h2>Schema governance</h2><p>Compatibility is reliability.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>Producer → compatibility check → Kafka → Consumer</code></pre><p>Carry event ID, event type, schema version, event time, and tracing context.</p></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section split-section" id="ka-replay"><div><div class="guide-title"><span>14</span><div><h2>Replay strategy</h2><p>Separate normal work from recovery work.</p></div></div><pre class="math-block"><code>Kafka ─┬→ production consumer group<br>       └→ replay consumer group</code></pre><p>Replay side effects only with idempotent processing.</p></div><div id="ka-controlplane"><div class="guide-title"><span>15</span><div><h2>Control plane vs data plane</h2><p>Policy stays off the event path.</p></div></div><div class="plane-compare"><span><b>Control</b> registry · quotas · placement · capacity · observability</span><span><b>Data</b> ingestion → Kafka → processing → downstreams</span></div><p>The control plane decides how the data plane behaves; it does not process the stream.</p></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-avoid"><div class="guide-title"><span>16</span><div><h2>What not to do</h2><p>Avoid answers that hide the real trade-off.</p></div></div><div class="avoid-grid"><span>Give every tenant a dedicated cluster by default.</span><span>Assume adding brokers instantly solves a short burst.</span><span>Use global ordering as the default requirement.</span><span>Autoscale consumers without protecting downstreams.</span><span>Claim exactly-once external side effects without idempotency.</span><span>Run the fleet at 90–100% steady-state utilization.</span></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-questions"><div class="guide-title"><span>17</span><div><h2>Interview follow-up drill</h2><p>Open each question and answer it aloud.</p></div></div><div class="deep-dive-list"><details open><summary><span>Why Kafka quotas instead of a gateway?</span><em>use both</em></summary><p>Kafka quotas protect broker resources. A gateway applies business policy, prioritizes events, routes tenants, and exposes tenant-aware admission control.</p></details><details><summary><span>Why multiple clusters?</span><em>blast radius</em></summary><p>They isolate noisy tenants, reduce failure scope, and allow independent capacity and lifecycle management.</p></details><details><summary><span>How many partitions?</span><em>derive from measurements</em></summary><p>Use measured sustainable partition throughput, add headroom, and validate failure and recovery behavior.</p></details><details><summary><span>How do you handle a hot customer?</span><em>ordering vs scale</em></summary><p>Keep one partition and accept its ceiling, or shard the key and restore only the ordering semantics the business needs.</p></details><details><summary><span>Why not exactly-once everywhere?</span><em>scope the guarantee</em></summary><p>Prefer at-least-once transport plus idempotent processing by default; use stronger guarantees where coordination cost is justified.</p></details><details><summary><span>What if every buffer fills?</span><em>admission policy</em></summary><p>Throttle, backpressure, prioritize critical events, spill when appropriate, or reject best-effort traffic. No system offers infinite buffering.</p></details><details><summary><span>When do you promote a tenant?</span><em>sustained evidence</em></summary><p>Use ingress rate, throttle time, partition hotness, lag, utilization, SLA class, and duration thresholds—not one spike.</p></details><details><summary><span>How do you protect the database?</span><em>bound the drain rate</em></summary><p>Bound concurrency, rate-limit, batch, bulkhead, and keep Kafka as the durable buffer. Consumer scaling must respect downstream capacity.</p></details></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-answer"><div class="guide-title"><span>18</span><div><h2>The 2-minute architect answer</h2><p>A complete close for the whiteboard discussion.</p></div></div><blockquote class="architect-answer">I would treat this as a noisy-neighbor and burst-management problem rather than simply a Kafka scaling problem. I would introduce tenant-aware admission control plus Kafka client quotas, use a tiered tenancy model, and isolate high-volume or highly bursty tenants onto dedicated topic pools or clusters.<br><br>Short bursts use backpressure and buffering; sustained abnormal volume triggers policy-driven promotion. Consumer compute is isolated and downstream databases and APIs are protected with bulkheads and rate limits.<br><br>Partitioning follows the business key for per-key ordering, with an explicit hot-key strategy. Kafka provides hot retention and replay, object storage holds long-term history, and selected critical topics replicate asynchronously across regions against a measured RPO.</blockquote></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ka-links"><div class="guide-title"><span>19</span><div><h2>Industry references</h2><p>Primary documentation for the design claims.</p></div></div><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#design_quotas" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apache Kafka — Quotas</a><a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#multitenancy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apache Kafka — Multi-tenancy</a><a href="https://docs.confluent.io/kafka/design/quotas.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Confluent — Kafka quotas</a><a href="https://docs.confluent.io/cloud/current/clusters/client-quotas.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Confluent Cloud — Client quotas</a></div><p class="practice-order"><strong>Practice order:</strong> Requirements → Capacity math → Partitioning → Broker sizing → Multi-tenancy → Burst handling → Reliability → Consumer isolation → DR → Cost → Trade-offs.</p><p class="source-note">Adapted from your <a href="https://pranavnandedkar.github.io/StreamingDocs/kafka-architect-prep.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Kafka Architect Interview Prep</a>.</p></section>
  </article>`;
}
